diff --git a/libc-bottom-half/sources/descriptor_table.c b/libc-bottom-half/sources/descriptor_table.c
index 56d7816..0a8c801 100644
--- a/libc-bottom-half/sources/descriptor_table.c
+++ b/libc-bottom-half/sources/descriptor_table.c
@@ -15,6 +15,237 @@
 
 #define MINSIZE 8
 
+#ifdef __wasilibc_set_threads__
+#include <stdatomic.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+
+/* enclave/SET: per-thread file-descriptor NAMESPACES.
+ *
+ * Under shared-everything threads the descriptor table is thread-local (see
+ * <wasi/descriptor_table.h>): component resource handles index the handle
+ * table of ONE instance, and each thread has its own. But every thread's table
+ * used to hand out the lowest free INDEX, so thread A's fd 4 and thread B's
+ * fd 4 were different files with the same name — while musl's `FILE` objects,
+ * which live in the SHARED linear memory, stayed common to both. Passing an fd
+ * between threads therefore did not fail, it ALIASED: reproduced by writing a
+ * worker's buffered secret into the main thread's file, with every call
+ * returning success. Silent cross-thread data corruption is the worst failure
+ * mode available, and it was the default one.
+ *
+ * So an fd carries the identity of the thread that owns it:
+ *
+ *     fd = (namespace << SET_FD_NS_SHIFT) | index
+ *
+ * and a lookup whose namespace is not this thread's returns EBADF.
+ *
+ * The namespace is drawn from a MONOTONIC counter, never from a free list.
+ * That is the whole point and it is worth stating plainly: an earlier revision
+ * recycled a fixed set of slots at thread exit, which re-opened the exact
+ * aliasing hole this exists to close — a dead thread's fd became a *valid* fd
+ * naming a different object as soon as the next thread inherited its slot, and
+ * a `FILE` the dead thread left on the process-global `__ofl` list would flush
+ * its buffered bytes into that object on any later `exit()`. Recycling was
+ * deterministic, not racy, so it was reachable without any cooperation.
+ * Monotonic ids make a stale fd fail with EBADF instead, for 2^18 thread
+ * creations per component instance.
+ *
+ * Two deliberate choices remain:
+ *
+ *  - Indices 0, 1 and 2 are NOT tagged. They are stdin/stdout/stderr on every
+ *    thread by construction — the same underlying streams — so aliasing them
+ *    is the correct semantics, not a bug. It also keeps `write(1, ...)` and
+ *    `STDOUT_FILENO` working on a worker.
+ *  - The MAIN thread takes namespace 0, so its fds are numerically identical
+ *    to a single-threaded build's. Only workers get large numbers.
+ *
+ * The layout leaves 13 bits of index and 18 bits of namespace, and the largest
+ * representable fd is exactly `INT_MAX`. 8192 descriptors per thread is well
+ * past any real program, and it also tightens `MAX_DESCRIPTOR`: `dup2(fd, N)`
+ * grows the table to `N+1` entries, so the old 1 Mi bound let a guest ask for a
